**Output format:** ALWAYS wrap PR descriptions in a ```markdown code block so they can be copied directly.

**Brevity (default):** Keep the whole description skimmable in about 15 seconds. Aim for these limits unless the user asks for more:
- Issue: 1-3 sentences. State the bug and its impact; do not narrate the debugging journey or paste discovery data (counts, row names, queries) unless one concrete number aids review.
- Solution: 2-4 bullets. Name what changed; do not restate the issue.
- To Test: 3-7 numbered steps a non-dev QA person can follow without asking a developer for help.
- Screenshots: only for user-facing UI; one template table, real before/after when it is a visual bug.

Write the tightest version first. Only expand when the user requests detail. Even when asked for "anecdotal evidence", fold it into a single sentence or number, not a paragraph.

**Section headings (keep emojis):** Use these headings in order: `## 🐛 Issue`, `## ✏️ Solution`, `## 🔬 To Test`. When the change is user-facing UI, add `## 📸 Screenshots` after To Test (same heading as `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`). Do not drop the emojis, substitute plain text headings, or reorder these sections when editing or rewriting a PR description unless the user explicitly asks for a different format.

## 🔬 To Test (write for QA, not for developers)

**Audience:** A non-developer QA person. They know the product UI; they do not read code. Default to this audience on every PR. Only use developer-oriented steps (breakpoints, file paths, SQL, curl) when the change is literally untestable without them (e.g. a migration) — and even then keep the language as plain as possible.

**Do:**
- Tell them what to open, tap, or click, in order
- Say what they should see on screen when it works (and, when useful, what they should *not* see)
- Include setup that matters (logged in vs logged out, which app/tab, force-close and reopen if needed)
- Name screens the way users see them ("Give tab", "success page", "avatar")

**Don't:**
- Reference file paths, line numbers, function names, env vars, or breakpoints
- Tell them to run `yarn test`, `yarn vitest`, `jest`, or any automated test runner (CI runs those)
- Use shorthand only a developer would parse ("Anonymous browsing → Login → Logout flow")
- Assume they know what "token handoff", "session", or similar means — describe the visible result instead

**Good example (product change):**

```markdown
## 🔬 To Test

1. Open the Android app and make sure you are logged in
2. Tap the Give tab
3. Confirm the giving page loads already signed in (your avatar/profile is visible; you are not asked to verify your phone number)
4. Tap your avatar on the giving page and log out
5. Force-close the app (swipe it away from recent apps)
6. Reopen the app, stay logged in, and tap Give again
7. Confirm you are signed in automatically again — no phone number / code prompt
```

**Bad example (same change, developer-speak — never write this):**

```markdown
## 🔬 To Test

1. Set breakpoint at `useRedirectUrl.ts` - confirm token attached to `/r`
2. Hit Give tab WebView with `APOLLOS_WEB_URL` override
3. Verify `getToken` returns `['token', ...]` for custom host
4. Logout in webview → kill app → reopen → confirm session cookie set
```

## 📸 Screenshots (user-facing UI only)

Add this section when a person can see the change on screen (layout, copy, empty states, clipped menus, new controls). Skip it for migrations, API-only, or flag wiring with no visible UI.

Match the repo PR template tables. Drop columns that do not apply (admin-only desktop can omit Phone / Tablet). For a visual bug, prefer before / after:

```markdown
## 📸 Screenshots

| Desktop (before) | Desktop (after) |
| -------------- | --------------- |
| ![Before](https://files.catbox.moe/before.png) | ![After](https://files.catbox.moe/after.png) |
```

**Real captures only.** Run the product (local or staging) in a browser and shoot the actual screen. Playwright against the running app is fine. Do not generate, draw, or invent UI images. Do not reuse a marketing / locked-state screenshot as if it were the live editor.

**Before / after.** For a visual bug, capture the broken UI first (unfixed branch or `main`), then the fixed UI on this branch. Same viewport, same screen, same account.

**Never commit screenshot files.** Do not `git add` png/jpg/gif/webp/mp4, and do not put them under `.github/pr-assets`, `docs/`, or anywhere else on the PR branch. If they are already committed, delete them from the branch.

Host the files outside git, then paste public HTTPS URLs into the PR body. For CLI capture from the running app and headless attach to the PR, follow https://github.com/ApollosProject/apollos-skills/blob/main/skills/pr-proof/SKILL.md.

```bash
# Write captures to /tmp, not the repo
curl -sF "reqtype=fileupload" -F "fileToUpload=@/tmp/before.png" https://catbox.moe/user/api.php
# Fallback: curl -sF "file=@/tmp/before.png" https://0x0.st
```

GitHub's image proxy needs a URL anyone can fetch. Do not use Linear `uploads.linear.app` links (they 401 in the PR). Do not use `gh gist create` (it rejects binary files). Delete the local files after the URLs are in the description.

## GitHub callouts (when they apply)

Use GitHub alert syntax inside PR bodies to surface context reviewers might skim past. Do not add a callout to every PR — only when the content fits.

```markdown
> [!NOTE]
> Highlights information that users should take into account, even when skimming.

> [!TIP]
> Optional information to help a user be more successful.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Crucial information necessary for users to succeed.

> [!WARNING]
> Critical content demanding immediate user attention due to potential risks.

> [!CAUTION]
> Negative potential consequences of an action.
```

When to use which:

| Callout | Use for |
|---------|---------|
| NOTE | Stack order, related PRs, scope boundaries ("migration-only; code in #1234"), follow-up work elsewhere |
| TIP | Optional reviewer shortcuts (env var, church slug, feature flag) that make manual testing easier — put these in a TIP, not in the numbered QA steps |
| IMPORTANT | Merge/deploy order, prerequisites, or behavior reviewers must not miss |
| WARNING | Time-sensitive risk (prod deploy window, breaking change if steps skipped) |
| CAUTION | Irreversible or harmful actions (data migration order, do not run backfill before deploy) |

Place callouts after the relevant section (usually Issue or Solution), not instead of normal prose.

Example of good PR description format:

```markdown
## 🐛 Issue

Closes APO-4736

Anonymous users who log in mid-session lose their prior anonymous ID, so analytics cannot join pre-login and post-login activity.

## ✏️ Solution

- Preserve the custom anonymous ID across login
- Identify the user with the correct ID after OTP
- Reset Segment state on logout so the next anonymous session is fresh

## 🔬 To Test

1. Open the app while logged out and browse a few screens
2. Log in with a phone number / code
3. Confirm you land in the app as yourself (your name/avatar shows)
4. Log out
5. Confirm you are anonymous again (no name/avatar; login is offered)
```

Example with a callout (stacked PR):

```markdown
## 🐛 Issue

[APO-10047](https://linear.app/differential/issue/APO-10047)

Migration-only PR: renames `charges.source_transfer_id` to `transfer_id`.

> [!NOTE]
> Application code changes ship in stacked [#3447](https://github.com/.../pull/3447). Merge and deploy this PR before #3447.

## ✏️ Solution

Migration 320: `renameColumn` with `setDynamicMasking` (same pattern as migration 288).

## 🔬 To Test

1. On a staging/dev database, run the migration forward
2. Confirm the `charges` table shows a column named `transfer_id` (not `source_transfer_id`)
3. Run the migration backward
4. Confirm the column is named `source_transfer_id` again
```

Example with user-facing UI (screenshots hosted off-branch):

```markdown
## 🐛 Issue

Closes APO-11368

On Apps > Mobile > a tab > Display, the icon list is cut off by the Tab Setup card, or sits under the sticky header.

## ✏️ Solution

- Portal the icon list out of the scroll card
- Pin it to the trigger so it stays above the sticky header

## 🔬 To Test

1. Open Admin and go to Apps > Mobile
2. Select a tab, then expand Display
3. Click the icon next to the tab name
4. Confirm the full icon list is visible (not cut off, not hidden under "Tab Setup")

## 📸 Screenshots

| Desktop (before) | Desktop (after) |
| -------------- | --------------- |
| ![Before](https://files.catbox.moe/before.png) | ![After](https://files.catbox.moe/after.png) |
```

## Reviewer patch (when useful)

For PRs where reviewers need to **see or trigger the change** with minimal setup (e.g. feature flags, env-driven behavior, one-off UI), include a small patch they can apply:

1. Create a patch file in the repo root (e.g. `reviewer-test.patch`) with a minimal diff that exercises the change (e.g. wire up a flag and show a banner, or add a one-off log).
2. In **To Test**, paste the patch content wrapped in `<details>`/`<summary>` so it’s collapsible (summary = filename, e.g. `reviewer-test.patch`; body = fenced code block with the diff). Tell reviewers to save that block to the filename, run `git apply <patch-file>`, set any required env, restart, and what to expect.
3. Note that the patch is for testing only and should be reverted before merge.

In the PR body, wrap the patch in collapsible details so the diff doesn’t dominate the description: `<details>`, `<summary>filename.patch</summary>`, then a fenced code block (e.g. `diff`) containing the patch, then `</details>`.

**Use when:** The PR changes behavior that’s easier to verify by applying a small, reversible patch (feature flags, env vars, toggles).  
**Skip when:** The PR is trivial, the patch would be large or brittle, or normal "To Test" steps are enough.
